  "summary": "An architectural and interior design style that celebrates industrial materials and technology as aesthetic elements.",
  "description": "Features exposed structural elements, visible ductwork and pipes, glass curtain walls, and steel frameworks, reflecting a fascination with industrial processes and the machine aesthetic. Pioneered by architects like Norman Foster and Richard Rogers, it emerged from the late modernist movement and gained prominence with buildings like the Pompidou Centre.",
  "culturalContext": "High-tech interiors — the 1970s-80s \"inside-out\" school (Richard Rogers's Lloyd's atrium, Pompidou's gerberettes, Ettore Sottsass's early studios, Norman Foster's Reichstag dome) — made services the ornament: exposed ducts as primary color, cable trays as ceiling patterning, factory glazing as domestic windows. \"High-tech\" softened into loft-industrial default; its grammar (structure displayed, services celebrated) underlies every industrial-chic workspace and tech-campus interior today.",
